uh please say that joining me now is the  education secretary and supporter of  this trust that's james cleve james  cleveland good morning to you thanks so  much for being with us why won't liz  trust richie soon boris johnson whoever  else sit down now to try and put anxious  families at ease about the prices  they're going to see come october well  the government the chancellor and the  business secretary have hauled in the  leaders of the energy companies to hold  them to account to do to discuss with  them what they are going to do with  these unexpected unplanned unprecedented  profits that they have been making  because of that sudden spike in energy  prices caused by russia's invasion of  ukraine the candidate's been putting  forward their plans liz has announced  that she will be  putting a temporary moratorium on the  green levees that push up  energy bills to an extent she's also  said she won't be going ahead with  national insurance rise that will put  forward  that will put back 240 pounds of  national insurance rise best part of 200  pounds into the pockets of people  and also what she said she is going to  do is bring forward an emergency budget  as soon as the house  comes back to address these issues  before the prices of energy uh go up  when the price cap is is is lifted um in  october you know this reported that many  families on the lowest incomes can have  a shortfall of some sixteen hundred  pounds now you talk about the chancellor  and the deems of hawaii and uh  quasi-quality business actually speaking  to the energy companies today is that an  acceptance then that the whimple tax  that the former chancellor put in was  inadequate  well we have already got a support  package in place a very  very very significant unprecedentedly  large support package in place we  recognize that prices are  are projected to go up  higher than the initial projections and  so that's why  liz has said that she will look at that  that's best done through an emergency  budget that's not something you can do  through recall parliament we have to do  that we have a new prime minister when  the house comes back in september we can  do that  before those prices go up uh in october  as we know they inevitably will yeah but  what i'm pushing one is this windfall  tax idea you are here i know you were  supporting this trust but you were also  government minister so i do want to get  an answer from the government because we  haven't heard from them very much in the  past few days so is this an acceptance  that the whimple tax that was put in  place with those carve outs to try and  encourage investment was wrong was was  misplaced and we need to go further look  what we're seeing is that the energy  cost the whole global energy costs are  still increasing faster now we need to  increasing higher so we need to respond  to that now the decisions that were made  at the time were the right decisions i  have no doubt about that but the  situation ukraine has uh dragged out the  energy cost implications have uh have  changed and of course as a responsible  government we we constantly need to  review the situation but of course the  other thing that we need to do which uh  doesn't get talked about so much is to  is to try and bring these prices these  wholesale energy prices back down again  that's why it's so important when we  deal in my old job and what liz is doing  as foreign secretary is to make sure  those energy generating countries those  oil and gas producing countries uh  increase production to to try and bring  those prices down and we've seen the  prices starting to come down  it won't filter through to people's  pockets as quickly as we would like and  that's why as i say liz has said she  will bring forward an emergency budget  as soon as we get back in september  ahead of those price rises in october  yeah and i think a deal has been signed  to bring in gas from the united states  as well in the past few days which  hopefully will help now actually move on  to your brief as well as education  secretary i know you knew in the gig um  but  schools are going to really struggle  this winter to be able to heat  the buildings and also feed the kids i  mean what's going to be done to make  sure that those schools can meet the  energy needs that they will have well  and this is why and i know you know  gordon brown who frankly should know  better has been telling everyone oh we  just recall parliament all these  problems go away it is a much more  complicated and therefore the government  answer needs to be a more comprehensive  answer than just come back for an  emergency debate in the summer because  you're right these energy price  pressures are hitting everyone both at  home  and businesses but of course as you're  right  as you say in my department this is  hitting schools universities  colleges  and we do need to look at what we can do  about that but it needs to be done it  needs to be done as an overall package  and it needs to be done with the  ultimate intention which is to bring  these costs down you can't just keep  chasing higher costs because we've got  to remember there's no such thing as  government money so whatever support  package we put in whether it be to  individuals or to organizations like  schools and colleges need to be paid for  and it's paid for out of taxation and  everyone loves the idea of more taxation  because they all think someone ever  because they're always not everywhere  they always think someone else is going  to pay you could borrow him so but let's  let's look i want to focus in on this  schooling of course i  apologize look the department for  education put out a survey early in the  year asking head teachers about the  costs that they would be incurring that  then was  the results of it were deferred in july  because of political uncertainties i  think it's probably when you were coming  into the job so when are teachers going  to know the amount of money that they're  going to expect because there was a  promise that there would be a little  extra funding for schools is that still  going to come are they going extra  funding well we uh we had a funding  announcement just before i stepped into  the  the job it was a it was a good funding  uh package but we absolutely recognize  that the costs have still continued to  ratchet up in large part because of this  invasion of ukraine and as i say  an emergency budget is the best place  one of those kind of set-piece fiscal  events is one of the best place to look  at the whole range of  income and expenditure within government  that's the right way to do that  we are absolutely looking at those costs  that schools and colleges and  universities uh are having to deal with  but as i say one of the the best ways of  doing this is to bring those costs down  and that's not that's that's not  happening this short term as you as you  readily admit and you and you could say  that you are going to make sure that  schools have adequate funding to make  sure that the buildings are warm enough  for the kids this just well of course  we've got a we've got to ensure that  schools can teach and the colleges can  teach and universities can teach we've  also got to make sure as i say that we  do we we whatever we do has to be done  in an economically sustainable way and i  know that it is it you know it is  disconcerting of course it is  for very worried  i totally get that i totally get that  and everyone's going to be feeling and  we are going to respond to that but as i  say the most sustainable way of doing  that is to push down those prices and  that's going to take international  effort as well as domestic politicians  should the uk be braced for blackouts  come january  well  we've got to understand that we we are  in a global  market we're in a global energy market  and the things which are affecting us  are affecting everyone around the world  we're in a better position than many in  terms of our domestic  energy production um and  you know there is every reason to  believe that we can get through this and  it's not going to be easy but we are a  resilient we've seen this through uh  through the covid situation we are  resourceful  resilient  agile uh country will continue to be so  yeah i mean you saw how badly going  covered so it could be this bad and we  could have blackouts come this winter no  i'm not saying that at all that's that's  what you're saying that's not what i was  saying the point i'm making is that um  we recognize the challenges that are  coming we will ensure that we plan  appropriately for what our global  circumstances including as you said  ensuring that there is a uh an overall  increase in production where we can  influence our international partners to  do so to make sure that we are able to  uh to continue being active as a country  and as an economy okay james cleveland  education security could speak to you  thank you so much

